// kyoten::platform — the platform seam (target-platforms § Decided, questions 1 and 2 of 2026-11-07, and 2026-11-20;
// the first slice's step 7, KYR-8): the `Platform` contract the engine runs a game through, the loop that runs the
// game's world on std::app (`play`), the three options every game's run reads from its command line, and the open
// platforms, one declaration per target.
//
// - **It is the engine's platform module,** which its records file marks (`"platform": true`), so the one place in the
//   engine whose code may name a platform flag. A game names none, and asks for capabilities.
// - **Tier `Module`, outside the simulation's fence,** as `kyoten::content` is: the loop reads the host's clock, the
//   window and the command line, which no simulation module may. It names `kyoten::frame` alone of the engine, so
//   `kyoten::frame` stays fenced for the `Time` a game's processors take.
// - **Calls are resolved at compile time:** `play` is generic over the world and the platform, so kama writes a copy
//   for each and calls it directly (target-platforms question 2). A closed platform is a private package's type
//   implementing the same contract, with its own module marked as this one is.

import { std::collections::DynamicArray, kyoten::frame::Sample, kyoten::frame::CursorMode };

export { Platform };

// What the engine asks of a platform, as far as the first slice's steps 7 and 8 need it: the content folders, the
// command line, the window and its input, the frame's timestamp and the end. Capabilities, the GPU and audio join it at
// the steps that need them.
type contract Platform for resource {
    // Where a package's content folder is in this build. `native` is the folder the generator found, which a native
    // development build reads; on the web, where the package's `preloadFiles` packed it, `/<package>/content`.
    const fn string contentFolder(const ref string package, const ref string native);
    // The command line after the program's name: on the web the page's query. The run reads its three options from it
    // (`RunOptions`), and leaves the rest to the game.
    const fn DynamicArray<string> arguments();
    // The game's window, `width` × `height` logical, titled `title`: a headless one when `headless` says so, taking the
    // focus only when `focused` says so. False when it cannot be opened, with why printed.
    fn bool openWindow(const ref string title, int32 width, int32 height, bool headless, bool focused);
    // The window's events since the last tick, its input added to `into` as samples, in the order of their times, each
    // on `now`'s base (KYR-54, step 8): false once it is closing.
    fn bool pumped(ref DynamicArray<Sample> into);
    // This frame's timestamp, in nanoseconds from any fixed start: the host's frame time, where an XR platform answers
    // its predicted display time.
    fn int64 now();
    // Player `player`'s text entry turned on or off, as the world asked after a frame: on, the window's text input, its
    // IME and an on-screen keyboard where no physical one is (design/input.md § Decided, 2026-11-25, the fifth part's
    // question 2).
    fn void textEntry(int32 player, bool on);
    // Player `player`'s cursor asked to be `mode`, as the world asked after a frame (design/input.md § Decided,
    // 2026-11-26, the sixth part's question 2): set at once, and while it asks `Locked`, a lock the window drops by
    // itself (Escape or a tab switch on the web, a focus loss natively) asked for again, as the platform allows.
    fn void cursor(int32 player, CursorMode mode);
    // The run ends with `code`, from inside the loop. Natively `run` then returns it; on the web, where `run` never
    // returns, the platform ends the program.
    fn void ended(uint8 code);
}
